content.text:
Here's the full Java surface, end to end. All of this is already implemented on this branch and proven by tests.

## 1. Roll a theme (global `SET_DESIGN_TOKEN` overrides)

`com.pathland.view.Theme` — base values are light, `dark*` builders emit the `dark.` variant:

```java
import com.pathland.view.Theme;

Theme theme = new Theme()
        .color("color.primary", 0xFF2563EB)        // light primary
        .darkColor("color.primary", 0xFF60A5FA)    // dark primary
        .color("color.surface", 0xFFFFFFFF)
        .darkColor("color.surface", 0xFF111827)
        .f32("space.base", 4.0f)                   // generative spacing base
        .string("font.body.family", "Inter");      // STRING-valued override
```

## 2. Reference tokens in a view (`Color.token(...)`)

```java
import com.pathland.view.Color;
import com.pathland.view.ForegroundStyle;
import com.pathland.view.Background;
import com.pathland.view.Border;
import com.pathland.view.Tint;
import com.pathland.view.Text;

Text.of("Accent text").modifier(ForegroundStyle.of(Color.token("color.primary")));
Rectangle.of().modifier(Background.of(Color.token("color.surface")));
Button.of("Go", onClick).modifier(Tint.of(Color.token("control.accent")));
```

These emit the `DESIGN_TOKEN` value type (`0x08`) — the path rides the frame's string section/arena. `Color.token` also works with a `dark.` prefix, e.g. `Color.token("dark.color.surface")`.

## 3a. SSR / WebSocket path — hand the theme to the `Emitter`

The theme's overrides ride the **mount** frame (so the SSR HTML document carries `:root{--pl-color-primary:…}` + the dark media query) and the resync frame. This is exactly what the Quarkus and Spring demos do:

```java
FrameOpcodeSink sink = new FrameOpcodeSink();     // or a WebSocket-fed sink
Emitter emitter = new Emitter(sink, theme);       // theme emitted at frame start
RenderResult result = emitter.mount(root, new Environment(state));

// SSR HTML now contains, e.g.:
//   :root{--pl-color-primary:rgba(37,99,235,1);}
//   @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){:root{--pl-color-primary:rgba(96,165,250,1);}}
//   ... <span style="color:var(--pl-color-primary)">Accent text</span>
```

The JS DOM client re-themes under `prefers-color-scheme: dark`; a native GTK renderer re-resolves under the GTK dark variant — no re-emission either way.

## 3b. Desktop / ring path (JNA into `libpathland_core`) — standalone

Overrides are renderer-global, so they can be one frame of their own:

```java
RingOpcodeSink ring = new RingOpcodeSink();
theme.emit(ring);                                  // one frame of SET_DESIGN_TOKEN
new Emitter(ring).mount(root, Environment.DEFAULT);
```

## Live examples in the repo

- `lib/java/pathland-demo-views/.../DemoTheme.java` — the rolled theme
- `.../ThemeSection.java` — the token-referencing view (`color.primary`, `control.accent`, `color.surface`, `dark.color.surface`, `control.background`)
- `pathland-quarkus-demo` + `pathland-spring-boot-demo` `SessionApp` — `new Emitter(sink, DemoTheme.theme())`
- Tests: `KitchenSinkViewTest.themeOverridesAndTokenRefsRideTheMountFrame`, `EmitterTest.themeEmitsGlobalDesignTokenOverrides`, `EmitterTest.tokenColorEmitsDesignTokenValueTypeWithPath`

Run the SSR demo to see it live: `cd lib/java/pathland-quarkus-demo && mvn quarkus:dev`, then toggle the OS/browser dark mode.

Both decisions locked in: the interface is **`ThemeData`**, and Rust gets mirrored. Final plan:

## Java

**`ThemeData.java` (new interface, `com.pathland.view`)**
```java
public interface ThemeData {
    void emitInto(OpcodeSink sink);                 // into an open frame
    default void emit(OpcodeSink sink) {            // one frame
        sink.beginFrame();
        emitInto(sink);
        sink.endFrame();
    }
}
```

**`Theme.java`** → `implements ThemeData`
- Keep single-value builders only: `color(path, argb)`, `f32`, `u32`, `u8`, `string`.
- **Remove** `darkColor`, `darkF32`, `darkString` and the `dark()` helper.
- `emitInto` pushes each override; make `Override` + an `overrides()` accessor package-private so `AdaptiveTheme` can read the dark theme's triples.

**`AdaptiveTheme.java` (new)**
```java
public final class AdaptiveTheme implements ThemeData {
    private final Theme light, dark;
    public AdaptiveTheme(Theme light, Theme dark) { ... }
    public void emitInto(OpcodeSink sink) {
        light.emitInto(sink);
        for (Override o : dark.overrides())
            sink.setDesignToken(dark(o.path()), o.valueType(), o.value());  // prefixes "dark."
    }
}
```
(Double-prefix guarded by the existing `startsWith("dark.")` check.)

**`Emitter.java`** — field + constructor param become `ThemeData`; `emitTheme()` calls `theme.emitInto(sink)` (unchanged behavior).

**Usages** — `DemoTheme` becomes `light()` / `dark()` / `adaptive()`; both `SessionApp`s and `KitchenSinkViewTest` use `new Emitter(sink, DemoTheme.adaptive())`.

**Tests** — rework `themeEmitsGlobalDesignTokenOverrides` to build light+dark `Theme`s + `AdaptiveTheme`, asserting base overrides emit unprefixed and dark ones as `dark.…`; add a `ThemeData`/light-only emit case.

## Rust (`pathland-engine`)

- `theme.rs`: **remove** `dark_color`, `dark_f32`, `dark_string`, `push_dark`. Add:
  ```rust
  #[derive(Debug, Clone, Default)]
  pub struct AdaptiveTheme { pub light: Theme, pub dark: Theme }
  impl AdaptiveTheme { pub fn new(light: Theme, dark: Theme) -> Self { ... } }
  ```
- `engine.rs`: extract `emit_token_override(path, &TokenValue, guest)` (shared Str-arena + numeric path); `apply_theme` uses it for light; new `apply_adaptive_theme(adaptive, guest)` emits light then dark-prefixed.
- Tests: `dark_builder_prepends_dark_prefix` becomes an `AdaptiveTheme` prefix test; add `apply_adaptive_theme` opcode assertions (paths `color.primary` + `dark.color.primary`).
- `pathland-view` (Rust) re-exports `AdaptiveTheme` alongside `Theme`.

## Docs/status
- `lib/java/pathland-view/status.md` — Theme bullet → `ThemeData` interface, single-value `Theme`, `AdaptiveTheme(light, dark)`.
- `pathland-engine/status.md` + `lib/rust/crates/pathland-view/status.md` — same model (Rust).

## Verification
